\layout Title

Network Performance Analysis --- Tutorial 3
\newline 
Erlang Delay and Loss and 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 Queue
\layout Author

Neil Lawrence
\layout Date

November 1st, 2004
\layout Enumerate

A telephone call centre services complaints from bank customers about the
 quality of their service.
 They expect at `busy hour' calls to arrive at a rate of 120 customers an
 hour.
 Dealing with each customer takes on average 3 minutes.
 When all serving assistants are occupied customers are placed in a holding
 system.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

The service center has 10 serving assistants.
 What is the probability of a customer having to join the queue? 
\layout Enumerate

What is the expected queueing time for each customer? 
\layout Enumerate

Given that customers who don't have to queue have an average queueing time
 of 0, what is the average waiting time for those who do have to queue?
 
\layout Enumerate

The bank wishes to combine two of these service centers for efficiency.
 Both service centers have arrival rates of 120 customers an hour in `busy
 hour'.
 How many staff will they need at the new centre to maintain a queueing
 probability at least as low as in a)? 
\layout Enumerate

Answer parts b) and c) for the new system.
 
\layout Standard

For this question you may assume that arrivals are Poisson distributed and
 service times are exponentially distributed.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

A telephone company plans to rent trunk lines between Birmingham and London
 for $1,000 per year.
 They expect to have a demand of 20 calls per hour, and an average holding
 time of 15 minutes.
 They can charge 0.01$/minute for each customer.
 They will provide no holding facility.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

How many trunk lines will they need to keep the blocking probability below
 0.01? 
\layout Enumerate

How much profit will they make? 
\layout Standard

For this question you may assume that arrivals are Poisson distributed and
 service times are exponentially distributed.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

A service time distribution is defined as follows: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
P(X)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
\frac{2}{b}-\frac{2}{b^{2}}X & 0<X\leq b\\
0 & X>b\label{eqn:distribution}\end{array}\right.\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

Sketch this distribution.
 
\layout Enumerate

The Pollaczek-Khinchin formula states the average waiting time for a queue
 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho)}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Enumerate

By determining the first two moments, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

, of the distribution given in 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eqn:distribution}

\end_inset 

 derive the average waiting time for the queue.
 
\the_end
